# WebKit Ruby baseline: independent bounded review

Base b29a420480b94d34876fe39a7451a446aadb240d. Read production CSS, real-output corpus producer, browser runner and spec20 directly. No production edits. The baseline-source fallback was independently copied while uncommitted; `after/doc.css` is the exact reviewed stylesheet. This is preliminary CSS review, not approval of subsequent runner/spec/CI changes.

The archived `conformance/results/doc-local-html/export/original-browser-input.log` supplies seven actual production HTML cases. The first attempted input was `backend/browser-raw-corpus.log`, which was actually a failed historical browser receipt rather than corpus input; it is retained as corpus-wrong-receipt.log. The extraction rejection is expected and not a browser result. The corrected input is frozen in corpus.log.

Independent Playwright 1.62.0 / Windows executions with document JavaScript disabled: Chromium 151.0.7922.34 and Firefox 153.0 had zero misalignments in 63 original cases; WebKit 26.5 had 54 failures. At 20px Arial normal line-height, Ruby base was 21.5px below reference A and nested Ruby base was 43px below. The seven-line inline-table/caption fallback produces zero failures in all three engines for the same 63 cases. Four separately identified CSS-only multiline diagnostics add 36 cases per engine and preserve the established first/last choice: Ruby publishes its base's final line, Anno its first, and a nested child's own exported baseline remains intact. These extra DOM variants are not claimed as newly compiled Doc inputs.

The fallback uses Ruby base inline-block inside the inline table, while Anno base is table-cell. This distinction is material: swapping them can select the wrong line for multiline content. Top and bottom captions keep annotation height in flow without fixed offsets or script geometry. All seven real-case Playwright aria snapshots are unchanged before/after in all three engines. Five additional CSS-only narrow-width, long-reading, two-note and following-line probes found no annotation/next-line overlap after the fix. These are limited automated accessibility snapshots and geometry checks, not VoiceOver/NVDA, selection/copy, keyboard-navigation, print or general accessibility certification. The first access.py helper searched for C directly before the paragraph close and failed on the existing wrapper span; access.initial.py preserves that incorrect helper, corrected without changing production.

Required regression scope for the final change:

- Keep seven existing real-output cases and add actual-parser multiline Ruby-last/Anno-first, Ruby in reading, Ruby in notes/multiple notes, and Anno-in-Anno; use fixed markers so every check asserts its exact intended baseline rather than the first matching descendant.
- Keep 12/20/32px and normal/1.2/2 line-height, nesting, table/list, scriptless output, finite emitted text and source contents. Test WebKit as well as Chromium/Firefox. A failed CSS.supports check must select the fallback, not itself fail a correct behavioral test.
- Measure annotation/base separation, line-box height, and later lines; transform or negative offsets can align a glyph while silently losing space reservation. Do not hide or arbitrarily split max-content annotations to conceal horizontal overflow.
- Retain source order base then reading/notes and every note. CSS visual reordering must not become document/source reordering, aria-hidden annotation removal, or duplicate text. Existing span markup does not gain native ruby semantics merely through this fallback.
- Bind final CSS/spec/corpus/runner hashes and browser versions. Do not infer all Safari platforms from Windows Playwright WebKit; printing, user styles/zoom, vertical writing and screen-reader behavior remain separate tests.

Primary references consulted: [CSS Grid container baselines](https://www.w3.org/TR/css-grid-1/#grid-baselines), [Grid reordering and accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/css-grid-1/#order-accessibility), and [CSS Inline baseline-source](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-inline/#baseline-source). They distinguish first/last baseline selection and visual layout from source-driven reading/navigation order. Browser support is established by the recorded executions, not by a standards citation.

No blocking semantic or layout regression was reproduced in the frozen fallback. The final producer/runner additions are still root WIP at this review boundary and need their own exact-input validation.
